## 📅 Month 3: Inference, LLMOps, Optimization, & Production Observability

*The focus is on serving models with ultra-low latency, running LLMs at scale, and keeping cloud costs under control.*

### Week 9: Production Model Inference & Serving

* **Day 57:** Inference Engines vs. Web Frameworks – Why Flask/FastAPI are terrible for model inference at scale.
* **Day 58:** Triton Inference Server – Setting up NVIDIA Triton to serve multiple frameworks (PyTorch, ONNX, TensorRT) concurrently.
* **Day 59:** Dynamic Batching – Configuring serving engines to group incoming real-time requests to maximize GPU throughput.
* **Day 60:** Model Compilers: TensorRT & ONNX – Optimizing and compiling models to cut latency by up to 5x.
* **Day 61:** KServe / Seldon Core – Deploying cloud-native serverless inference platforms on Kubernetes.
* **Day 62:** Canary Deployments for AI – Setting up Istio or Linkerd to route 5% of live traffic to a new model version to test accuracy.
* **Day 63:** Weekly Review – Deploy an optimized ONNX model on Triton Inference Server with dynamic batching turned on.

### Week 10: LLMOps (Large Language Models in Production)

* **Day 64:** LLM Serving Challenges – Understanding Autoregressive generation, Time-to-First-Token (TTFT), and Inter-Token Latency.
* **Day 65:** vLLM – Deploying an open-source LLM (e.g., Llama 3) utilizing PagedAttention to optimize memory.
* **Day 66:** TGI (Text Generation Inference) – Exploring Hugging Face’s highly optimized production LLM stack.
* **Day 67:** Model Quantization Standards – Deep dive into AWQ, GPTQ, and GGUF to shrink models from 16-bit to 4-bit precision.
* **Day 68:** RAG Infrastructure – Building production scaffolding to connect text extraction, chunking, vector indexing, and LLM APIs.
* **Day 69:** Multi-LoRA Serving – Serving hundreds of custom, fine-tuned models on a single base LLM engine without reloading weights.
* **Day 70:** Weekly Review – Host a quantized Llama model using vLLM on Kubernetes, measuring the TTFT and total requests handled per second.

### Week 11: Monitoring, Observability, & AI Guardrails

* **Day 71:** Prometheus & Grafana for AI – Scraping specific hardware metrics (NVIDIA DCGM metrics) and inference metrics.
* **Day 72:** Tracing LLM Apps – Setting up OpenInference Tracing and LangSmith/Arize Phoenix to see exactly where a prompt stalls.
* **Day 73:** LLM Cost Monitoring – Building dashboards to calculate token consumption, pricing, and cost per user request.
* **Day 74:** Concept Drift & Accuracy Monitoring – Monitoring system inputs for semantic drift over time using tools like Evidently AI.
* **Day 75:** NeMo Guardrails – Implementing programmatic validation walls to prevent jailbreaks, prompt injections, and profanity.
* **Day 76:** Llama Guard – Running content moderation models in your pipeline to inspect both user inputs and model outputs.
* **Day 77:** Weekly Review – Create a Grafana dashboard that visualizes concurrent user requests, total token spend, and average GPU VRAM temperature.

### Week 12: FinOps, AI Security, & GitOps Automation

* **Day 78:** FinOps for AI – Analyzing spot instance strategies, autoscaling delays, and pricing models across cloud providers.
* **Day 79:** Model Supply Chain Security – Scanning model weights for malware or malicious code execution (e.g., switching pickle files to Safetensors).
* **Day 80:** RBAC & IAM for AI Assets – Limiting access to sensitive datasets, API tokens, and production model registries.
* **Day 81:** Local AI Agents for DevOps – Integrating toolkits like Claude Code or Cursor into your pipeline for self-healing error logs.
* **Day 82:** GitOps for AI – Implementing ArgoCD to handle deployments of model infrastructure configurations.
* **Day 83:** CI/CD for Models (CT Pipelines) – Setting up continuous training pipelines that trigger automatically on data drift notifications.
* **Day 84:** Comprehensive System Architecture Review – Designing an end-to-end cloud infrastructure topology for enterprise AI.

